<urn:atm:triage:finding/RSH-001>
  a triage:Finding ;
  triage:findingId "RSH-001" ;
  triage:title "Peer HTTPS connection-admission check-then-act race in accept_loop allows unbounded worker-thread spawn past the configured cap" ;
  triage:description "In crates/atm-daemon/src/https_transport.rs, accept_loop (~lines 415-468) calls peer_connection_admission(stop_flag, requests.active_connections()) at line ~425-428 to decide whether to admit a newly accepted TCP connection, then on PeerConnectionAdmission::Admit calls spawn_request_worker(stream, ...) (line 440). Inside spawn_request_worker (~lines 494-539), the connection counter is only incremented at line 518, inside the spawned worker thread's own closure (let _active = requests.register();), which runs asynchronously after std::thread::Builder::spawn returns. This is a classic check-then-act race: active_connections() is read in the accept loop thread, but the corresponding increment happens later, in a different thread, with no atomic reservation in between. Because this happens pre-TLS-authentication (before any peer identity check), a single malicious or misbehaving TCP peer can burst many connections in quick succession; each accept_loop iteration observes a stale (not-yet-incremented) active_connections() count and admits another connection, spawning an effectively unbounded number of OS worker threads before the counter catches up — defeating the intended MAX_PEER_HTTP_CONNECTIONS (64) cap." ;
  triage:phaseId "phase-AI" ;
  triage:triageMode "initial_pass" ;
  triage:category "rust_service_hardening_concurrency" ;
  triage:severity "important" ;
  triage:repeatable false ;
  triage:sweepScope "single site" ;
  triage:status "open" ;
  triage:dispatchReady true ;
  triage:triagedAt "2026-07-28T00:15:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
  triage:foundIn triage:AICH-S16 ;
